  "habitat": "Pairs in thorny thickets, often near water, feeding on open ground with other waxbills and firefinches.",
  "summary": "A common resident. Yellow-green mantle and wings, a red rump and upper tail, and black-and-white barred underparts. The male has a red forehead and throat. The bill is longer than Orange-winged Pytilia and predominantly red, and the wings are all green. The usual call is a low wick, with a plaintive whistle and a short song. The Kemp name is Melba Finch and the code RB was left unchanged.",
